body:
## Summary

`--capture api` emits a trailing two-space CommonMark hard break (`  \n`) on **almost every line**, including lines that should end a paragraph. The visible effect on rendered output: caption + image (or two adjacent paragraphs) become a single `<p>` with a `<br>` between them, instead of two separate paragraphs with normal spacing.

## Concrete reproduction

A captured file has lines that look (and even diff) like a normal paragraph break:

```
Caption A:  ⏎              <-- two trailing spaces, then newline (hard break)
![](images/x.png)  ⏎       <-- two trailing spaces, then newline (hard break)
  ⏎                         <-- "blank" line — but actually 5 spaces + 2 trailing spaces + \n
Caption B:  ⏎               <-- hard break
![](images/y.png)  ⏎        <-- hard break
```

(Run `cat -A` or `grep -P '  $'` to see them.)

### How a CommonMark renderer interprets it

Verified against `markdown-it-py` (CommonMark mode):

```
INPUT (current):
Caption A:  ⏎![](images/x.png)  ⏎  ⏎Caption B:  ⏎![](images/y.png)⏎

RENDERS AS:
<p>Caption A:<br />
<img src="images/x.png" alt="" /></p>
<p>Caption B:<br />
<img src="images/y.png" alt="" /></p>
```

vs the intended rendering (no trailing two-space):

```
RENDERS AS:
<p>Caption A:</p>
<p><img src="images/x.png" alt="" /></p>
<p>Caption B:</p>
<p><img src="images/y.png" alt="" /></p>
```

The difference is significant: in the current output, **every image is cramped against its caption with no vertical spacing**, because the renderer treats them as a single paragraph joined by `<br>`. In GitHub/GitLab/Pandoc/most static-site generators, the result is visibly broken layout — captions and images blur together into one block.

## Why this matters

- The user reads the markdown in a renderer (GitHub PR review, MkDocs site, Pandoc PDF). The layout is wrong everywhere.
- It also makes the markdown source unreasonable to edit by hand: a "blank" separator line that visually appears empty actually carries 5 spaces + 2 trailing spaces, so removing it accidentally breaks the document differently than expected.
- Diff tooling treats the trailing-space lines as content lines, polluting code review.

## Root cause hypothesis

The Rust HTML→Markdown converter (`html2md` family) emits a hard break (`  \n`) for every `<br>` in the source HTML — and Google Docs export wraps every visual line break in `<br>`, even at paragraph boundaries (because the source uses `<br><br>` as paragraph separators in places). The output preserves all of them as hard breaks instead of distinguishing `<br>` (single line wrap) from `<br><br>` (paragraph break).

The JS converter has the same pattern.

## Suggested fix

Treat consecutive `<br>` runs as a paragraph break, not a sequence of hard breaks:

- Single `<br>` → emit `  \n` (CommonMark hard break) — keep as-is.
- Two or more consecutive `<br>` → emit `\n\n` (paragraph break) — collapse to a real blank line.

Equivalent post-pass in pseudo-code:

```
md = md.replace(/(  \n){2,}/g, '\n\n')
```

(Strictly: replace runs of trailing-two-space-newline-then-trailing-two-space-newline with a single blank line.)

## Reproducible test (synthetic data)

### Shared HTML fixture — `tests/fixtures/paragraph-vs-line-break.html`

```html
<!doctype html>
<html><body>
<p>
  <span>Caption A:</span><br>
  <img alt="" src="data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgo="><br><br>
  <span>Caption B:</span><br>
  <img alt="" src="data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgo=">
</p>
</body></html>
```

### JS — `js/tests/unit/paragraph-vs-line-break.test.js`

```js
import { convertHtmlToMarkdown } from '../../src/lib.js';
import fs from 'fs';

const HTML = fs.readFileSync(__dirname + '/../fixtures/paragraph-vs-line-break.html', 'utf8');

it('renders a paragraph break (\\n\\n) for <br><br>, not two hard breaks', async () => {
  const md = await convertHtmlToMarkdown(HTML);
  // Caption A and Caption B must be in separate paragraphs — there must be at least one
  // empty line between their lines, with no trailing two-space on the empty line.
  const lines = md.split('\n');
  const idxA = lines.findIndex(l => l.includes('Caption A:'));
  const idxB = lines.findIndex(l => l.includes('Caption B:'));
  expect(idxA).toBeGreaterThanOrEqual(0);
  expect(idxB).toBeGreaterThan(idxA);

  const between = lines.slice(idxA + 1, idxB);
  // At least one truly empty line (no whitespace at all)
  expect(between.some(l => l === '')).toBe(true);

  // No "blank-looking" line that actually carries trailing whitespace
  expect(md).not.toMatch(/^[ \t]+ {2}$/m);
});

it('CommonMark renders captions and images as separate paragraphs', async () => {
  const md = await convertHtmlToMarkdown(HTML);
  const { default: MarkdownIt } = await import('markdown-it');
  const html = new MarkdownIt('commonmark').render(md);
  // Each caption is in its own <p>
  expect(html).toMatch(/<p>Caption A:[^<]*<\/p>/);
  expect(html).toMatch(/<p>Caption B:[^<]*<\/p>/);
});
```

### Rust — `rust/tests/integration/paragraph_vs_line_break.rs`

```rust
use web_capture::markdown::convert_html_to_markdown;

const HTML: &str = include_str!("../fixtures/paragraph-vs-line-break.html");

#[test]
fn double_br_becomes_paragraph_break_not_two_hard_breaks() {
    let md = convert_html_to_markdown(HTML).unwrap();
    let lines: Vec<&str> = md.lines().collect();
    let idx_a = lines.iter().position(|l| l.contains("Caption A:")).unwrap();
    let idx_b = lines.iter().position(|l| l.contains("Caption B:")).unwrap();
    let between = &lines[idx_a + 1..idx_b];
    assert!(
        between.iter().any(|l| l.is_empty()),
        "expected at least one truly empty line between Caption A and Caption B; got:\n{md}"
    );
}

#[test]
fn no_blank_looking_line_carries_trailing_whitespace() {
    let md = convert_html_to_markdown(HTML).unwrap();
    let bad: Vec<&str> = md.lines().filter(|l| !l.is_empty() && l.trim().is_empty()).collect();
    assert!(bad.is_empty(), "lines that look blank must actually be blank; offenders: {bad:?}");
}
```

Both tests fail today on captures from real Google Docs (the JS-API output reproducibly contains lines made of 5 spaces + 2 trailing spaces between captions) and pass once the converter coalesces `<br><br>` into `\n\n`.

## Summary

JS `--capture api` produces **structurally broken bold markup** because adjacent inline bold spans in the HTML are wrapped independently (instead of merged) and bold ranges leak across `<br>`/`<img>`/paragraph boundaries. Concrete symptoms in real captures:

```
Symptom A — adjacent bold runs not coalesced
  HTML:    <span class="c8">13.1 </span><span class="c8">First subsection heading</span>
  Output:  **13.1** **First subsection heading**       (two bold runs glued)
  Wanted:  **13.1 First subsection heading**            (one bold run)

Symptom B — stray empty bold pair "****"
  HTML:    ...end-of-list-item</span><span class="c8">Note:</span>...
  Output:  ...end-of-list-item)  ****Note:** ...        (****  =  unclosed outer + opener of inner)
  Wanted:  ...end-of-list-item)\n\n**Note:** ...

Symptom C — bold range spans across <br> and <img>
  HTML:    <span class="c8">Caption A:<img ...><br>Caption B:<img ...><br>...</span>
  Output:  **Caption A:![](images/x.png)Caption B:![](images/y.png)...**     (one giant bold run)
  Wanted:  **Caption A:**\n\n![](images/x.png)\n\n**Caption B:**\n\n![](images/y.png)\n...

Symptom D — bold opens on its own line
  HTML:    ...</li></ol><p><span class="c22"><br></span><span class="c8">13.2 ...</span>...
  Output:  **\n13.2 ...**Caption:![](images/x.png)**\n  (a "**" alone on a line, then bold leaks through image)
```

All four symptoms share a single root cause: the converter wraps each inline-bold span in `**...**` independently and never validates that the resulting `*` count is balanced or that bold ranges respect block boundaries.

## Why it matters

- **Renderers cannot interpret the output**: `****Note:**` is invalid CommonMark and renders as four literal asterisks followed by bold "Note:" with an unclosed bold trailing through the rest of the paragraph.
- **Bold leaks visually corrupt the document**: a single misplaced `**` propagates bolding across many subsequent paragraphs and images.
- **Heading-like content is split**: `**13.1** **Foo**` is two short bold runs that no renderer recognises as a heading; they look like two separate emphasised tokens with a space.

This is independent of #119 (`<br>` inside list items) — even after `<br>` handling is fixed, the bold-balancing problem remains.

## Root cause hypothesis

In `js/src/gdocs-preprocess.js::hoistInlineStyleSpans` (and the analogous Rust function in `rust/src/gdocs.rs::hoist_inline_style_spans`), every `<span style="font-weight:700">` (or class-mapped equivalent) is rewritten to `<strong>...</strong>`. Two adjacent `<strong>` siblings then both emit `**...**`, producing the `**a** **b**` pattern instead of `**a b**`.

A second pass should:

1. **Coalesce adjacent `<strong>` siblings** (separated only by whitespace text nodes) into a single `<strong>` before passing to the markdown converter.
2. **Refuse to open `<strong>` whose closing tag would land in a different block-level container** (cross `<p>`/`<li>`/`<br>`/`<img>` boundary). Either close it early at the boundary and reopen on the other side, or drop the styling on the boundary-spanning text.
3. **Strip empty `<strong></strong>`** before conversion (defensive).

## Reproducible test (synthetic data)

### Shared HTML fixture — `tests/fixtures/bold-coalesce-and-leakage.html`

```html
<!doctype html>
<html><body>
<!-- Symptom A: two adjacent bold spans -->
<p><span style="font-weight:700">13.1 </span><span style="font-weight:700">First subsection</span></p>

<!-- Symptom B: outer bold ends, inner bold begins, no separator -->
<ol>
  <li><span style="font-weight:700">Item header.</span></li>
</ol>
<p><span style="font-weight:700">Note:</span> body text.</p>

<!-- Symptom C: bold range spans across <br> and <img> -->
<p>
  <span style="font-weight:700">
    Caption A:<img alt="" src="data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgo="><br>
    Caption B:<img alt="" src="data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgo=">
  </span>
</p>
</body></html>
```

### JS — `js/tests/unit/bold-coalesce-and-leakage.test.js`

```js
import { convertHtmlToMarkdown } from '../../src/lib.js';
import fs from 'fs';

const HTML = fs.readFileSync(__dirname + '/../fixtures/bold-coalesce-and-leakage.html', 'utf8');

describe('bold-run coalescing and block boundary handling', () => {
  let md;
  beforeAll(async () => { md = await convertHtmlToMarkdown(HTML); });

  it('A: coalesces adjacent bold spans into a single bold run', () => {
    expect(md).toMatch(/\*\*13\.1 First subsection\*\*/);
    // The split form must NOT appear
    expect(md).not.toMatch(/\*\*13\.1\*\*\s+\*\*First subsection\*\*/);
  });

  it('B: never emits an empty "****" pair between adjacent bold runs', () => {
    expect(md).not.toMatch(/\*{4}/);
  });

  it('C: closes bold at block-level boundaries (<br>, <img>)', () => {
    // No bold run may contain an image syntax inside it.
    expect(md).not.toMatch(/\*\*[^*]*!\[[^\]]*\]\([^)]+\)[^*]*\*\*/);
    // Each caption must remain bold individually.
    expect(md).toMatch(/\*\*Caption A:\*\*/);
    expect(md).toMatch(/\*\*Caption B:\*\*/);
  });

  it('D: every "**" opener has a matching closer (balanced asterisks)', () => {
    // Strip code spans and image syntax, then count unescaped `**` — must be even.
    const stripped = md.replace(/`[^`]*`/g, '').replace(/!\[[^\]]*\]\([^)]*\)/g, '');
    const count = (stripped.match(/\*\*/g) || []).length;
    expect(count % 2).toBe(0);
  });
});
```

### Rust — `rust/tests/integration/bold_coalesce_and_leakage.rs`

```rust
use web_capture::markdown::convert_html_to_markdown;

const HTML: &str = include_str!("../fixtures/bold-coalesce-and-leakage.html");

#[test]
fn coalesces_adjacent_bold_spans() {
    let md = convert_html_to_markdown(HTML).unwrap();
    assert!(md.contains("**13.1 First subsection**"), "expected single bold run; got:\n{md}");
    assert!(!md.contains("**13.1** **First subsection**"), "must NOT split into two bold runs");
}

#[test]
fn never_emits_quad_asterisk() {
    let md = convert_html_to_markdown(HTML).unwrap();
    assert!(!md.contains("****"), "must not emit '****' (empty bold pair); got:\n{md}");
}

#[test]
fn closes_bold_at_block_boundaries() {
    let md = convert_html_to_markdown(HTML).unwrap();
    let re = regex::Regex::new(r"\*\*[^*]*!\[[^\]]*\]\([^)]+\)[^*]*\*\*").unwrap();
    assert!(!re.is_match(&md), "no bold run may contain an image; got:\n{md}");
    assert!(md.contains("**Caption A:**"));
    assert!(md.contains("**Caption B:**"));
}

#[test]
fn balanced_double_asterisks() {
    let md = convert_html_to_markdown(HTML).unwrap();
    let stripped = regex::Regex::new(r"`[^`]*`|!\[[^\]]*\]\([^)]*\)").unwrap()
        .replace_all(&md, "");
    let count = stripped.matches("**").count();
    assert_eq!(count % 2, 0, "expected even count of `**`; got {count} in:\n{md}");
}
```

All eight tests fail today on JS-API output (and several fail on Rust-API too, since the same `hoist_inline_style_spans` shape exists on both sides). Each passes once the converter is taught to coalesce adjacent bold siblings, close runs at block boundaries, and strip empties.

## Recommended fix order

1. Land the coalescing pass for adjacent `<strong>` siblings in both `gdocs-preprocess.js` and `gdocs.rs`. This kills symptoms A and B with one change.
2. Add a balanced-asterisks linter in the integration tests that runs against every captured fixture in `tests/fixtures/` to catch regressions.
3. Tighten the cross-block leakage by closing bold at `<br>`, `<img>`, `<p>` boundaries inside the preprocessor.

## Summary

JS `--capture api` (export-html → markdown) **collapses `<br>`-separated lines inside a list item or paragraph into a single run**, gluing them to the preceding text with no separator. The other three paths — Rust-API, JS-browser, Rust-browser — all produce one line per `<br>`, which is the user's intent.

## Reproduction

A small synthetic Google Doc whose body contains a single ordered-list item with a glossary block of `<br>`-separated definitions reproduces the bug. The doc structure is:

```
Glossary entry.
TAG1 – Definition one.       <-- separate line in source
TAG2 – Definition two.       <-- separate line in source
TAG3 – Definition three.     <-- separate line in source
```

(Anyone can recreate this: open a new Google Doc, type the four lines using Shift+Enter between them so they are `<br>`-separated rather than `<p>`-separated, set sharing to "Anyone with the link can view", and capture with `--capture api`.)

After capture with `--capture api --format markdown`, the four paths yield:

```
JS-API (BUG):
   **TAG1** – Definition one.**TAG2** – Definition two.**TAG3** – Definition three.

Rust-API (correct):
   **TAG1** – Definition one.
   **TAG2** – Definition two.
   **TAG3** – Definition three.

JS-browser (correct):    same shape as Rust-API
Rust-browser (correct):  same shape as Rust-API
```

JS-API concatenates the three definitions into one paragraph with no whitespace between them. The other three paths preserve one definition per line.

## Why this is its own issue (not #115 or #118)

- **#115** is about `--capture browser` dropping content (links/footnotes). This is the opposite pattern: `--capture api` keeps the content but glues lines.
- **#118** is about ordered-list numbering parity, not about line breaks.

## Root cause hypothesis

The JS converter (`node-html-markdown` or whichever wrapper) is likely configured to drop bare `<br>` inside list items or strong/inline contexts. The Rust converter (`html2md` family) treats `<br>` as a hard break (two trailing spaces + `\n`). Pick one canonical behaviour and align both.

Rust uses two-space hard breaks (`one.  \n   **TAG2**`), which is correct CommonMark. JS-API should do the same. The browser path on both sides emits plain `\n` between runs, which also renders correctly.

## Reproducible test (synthetic data)

### Shared HTML fixture — `tests/fixtures/list-item-with-br.html`

```html
<ol>
  <li><strong>Glossary entry.</strong>Some lead text.<br>
    <strong>TAG1</strong> – Definition one.<br>
    <strong>TAG2</strong> – Definition two.<br>
    <strong>TAG3</strong> – Definition three.
  </li>
</ol>
```

### JS — `js/tests/unit/html2md-br-in-list-item.test.js`

```js
import { convertHtmlToMarkdown } from '../../src/lib.js';
import fs from 'fs';

const HTML = fs.readFileSync(__dirname + '/../fixtures/list-item-with-br.html', 'utf8');

it('preserves <br> as a line separator inside list items', async () => {
  const md = await convertHtmlToMarkdown(HTML);
  // Each definition must end up on its own line — i.e. there must be a newline (or hard break)
  // between the end of one definition and the start of the next.
  for (const tag of ['TAG1', 'TAG2', 'TAG3']) {
    // The label appears at the start of a line (allowing for indentation and bold markers).
    expect(md).toMatch(new RegExp(`(^|\\n)\\s*\\*{0,2}${tag}\\*{0,2}\\s*–`, 'm'));
  }
  // No two definition labels may share a line.
  expect(md).not.toMatch(/Definition one\.\s*\*{0,2}TAG2/);
  expect(md).not.toMatch(/Definition two\.\s*\*{0,2}TAG3/);
});
```

### Rust — `rust/tests/integration/html2md_br_in_list_item.rs`

```rust
use web_capture::markdown::convert_html_to_markdown;

const HTML: &str = include_str!("../fixtures/list-item-with-br.html");

#[test]
fn preserves_br_as_line_separator_inside_list_items() {
    let md = convert_html_to_markdown(HTML).unwrap();
    for tag in ["TAG1", "TAG2", "TAG3"] {
        let re = regex::Regex::new(&format!(r"(?m)^\s*\*{{0,2}}{tag}\*{{0,2}}\s*–")).unwrap();
        assert!(re.is_match(&md), "expected `{tag} – …` on its own line; got:\n{md}");
    }
    assert!(!md.contains("Definition one.**TAG2"),  "TAG1/TAG2 must not be glued: {md}");
    assert!(!md.contains("Definition two.**TAG3"),  "TAG2/TAG3 must not be glued: {md}");
}
```

JS fails today (the `Definition one.**TAG2` glue assertion trips). Rust passes.

## Suggested fix

In the JS HTML→Markdown stage, treat `<br>` inside `<li>` (and inside any block-level container) as a hard line break: emit `  \n` (two spaces + newline). This matches CommonMark spec for hard breaks and matches what the Rust converter already does.

If the converter library does not support per-tag rule overrides easily, a small post-pass that re-introduces newlines around `<br>`-derived markers before passing to the converter would also work.

## Summary

For the same Google Docs HTML export, JS and Rust HTML→Markdown converters emit **different ordered-list numbering** even though the rendered output is the same:

| Converter | Style                                                                                | Example         |
|-----------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------|
| JS        | Continuous: `1.`, `2.`, `3.`, ... `N.` for every `<li>` in document order            | `1.` `2.` `3.` |
| Rust      | Per-list-restart: each new ordered list starts at `1.` regardless of `start=` attr   | `1.` `1.` `1.` |

```
$ grep -cE '^1\.' js-api/doc.md       # 1
$ grep -cE '^1\.' rust-api/doc.md     # 13
$ grep -cE '^2\.' js-api/doc.md       # 1
$ grep -cE '^2\.' rust-api/doc.md     # 6
```

## Why it matters

This violates the JS/Rust parity goal documented in `docs/case-studies/issue-36/README.md` R4 ("Both JavaScript and Rust implementations must support the same Google Docs capture functionality with **identical behavior**.").

It also breaks the **heading numbering** contract from issue 03 of this batch: a top-level heading numbered `13.` in the source becomes `3.` in Rust because the second-occurrence `<ol>` restarts the counter (1, 2, 3 across two lists totalling 13 items).

## Root cause hypothesis

JS uses `node-html-markdown` configured with a continuous counter that walks the DOM. Rust uses `html2md`/`scraper` whose default for `<ol>` is to restart at 1 unless the converter explicitly threads a counter or honours the `start=` attribute.

## Fix

Pick **one** canonical behaviour and align both. CommonMark + GFM render the same regardless, so this is a stylistic choice — but it must be the same on both sides.

Recommended: **continuous numbering** (JS today). It is more diff-friendly, makes manual cross-referencing easier, and matches what users see in the Google Docs UI.

If `<ol start="N">` is present, honour it on both implementations.

## Reproducible test (fake data)

### Shared HTML fixture — `tests/fixtures/ol-continuous-numbering.html`

```html
<ol>
  <li><strong>First</strong></li>
  <li><strong>Second</strong></li>
</ol>
<p>Some prose between lists.</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Third</strong></li>
  <li><strong>Fourth</strong></li>
</ol>
<ol start="13">
  <li><strong>Top-level numbered heading.</strong></li>
</ol>
```

### JS — `js/tests/unit/html2md-ol-numbering.test.js`

```js
import { convertHtmlToMarkdown } from '../../src/lib.js';
import fs from 'fs';

const HTML = fs.readFileSync(__dirname + '/../fixtures/ol-continuous-numbering.html', 'utf8');

it('numbers consecutive top-level ordered lists continuously', async () => {
  const md = await convertHtmlToMarkdown(HTML);
  expect(md).toMatch(/^\s*1\.\s+\*\*First\*\*/m);
  expect(md).toMatch(/^\s*2\.\s+\*\*Second\*\*/m);
  expect(md).toMatch(/^\s*3\.\s+\*\*Third\*\*/m);
  expect(md).toMatch(/^\s*4\.\s+\*\*Fourth\*\*/m);
  expect(md).toMatch(/^\s*13\.\s+\*\*Top-level/m);
});
```

### Rust — `rust/tests/integration/html2md_ol_numbering.rs`

```rust
use web_capture::markdown::convert_html_to_markdown;

const HTML: &str = include_str!("../fixtures/ol-continuous-numbering.html");

#[test]
fn numbers_consecutive_top_level_ordered_lists_continuously() {
    let md = convert_html_to_markdown(HTML).unwrap();
    assert!(md.contains("1. **First**"),               "got:\n{md}");
    assert!(md.contains("2. **Second**"),              "got:\n{md}");
    assert!(md.contains("3. **Third**"),               "got:\n{md}");
    assert!(md.contains("4. **Fourth**"),              "got:\n{md}");
    assert!(md.contains("13. **Top-level"),            "must honour <ol start=13>; got:\n{md}");
}
```

Today: Rust fails on items 3, 4, and 13 (it emits `1. 2. 1. 2. 1.`). JS passes the first four, fails on `13.` (it doesn't honour `start=`).

If the team prefers Rust's "restart per list" behaviour, invert the assertions and update JS — the point is **one** canonical scheme.

## Summary

`--keep-original-links` on `--capture api` for Google Docs strips every image without leaving a placeholder, because the export response uses `<img alt="" src="data:image/png;base64,...">` and the strip helper only emits a placeholder when alt is non-empty.

```
$ web-capture URL --capture api --keep-original-links --format markdown -o doc.md
Stripped 39 base64 images (keeping original links)     # success-looking message
$ grep -c '!\[' doc.md
0                                                       # …but every image reference is gone
```

Reproduces in JS 1.7.19 and Rust 0.3.11.

## Root cause

### JavaScript — `js/src/extract-images.js`

```js
export function stripBase64Images(markdown) {
  let stripped = 0;
  const updatedMarkdown = markdown.replace(
    BASE64_IMAGE_REGEX,
    (_match, altText) => {
      stripped++;
      return altText ? `*[image: ${altText}]*` : '';   // ← empty string when alt is empty
    }
  );
  return { markdown: updatedMarkdown, stripped };
}
```

### Rust — `rust/src/extract_images.rs::strip_base64_images`

Same shape, same outcome. Google Docs HTML export emits `alt=""` for every image, so the truthiness check kills every replacement.

## Why it matters

- The flag is dangerously inconsistent with its own help text. The help text promises images will be left as references; instead they are silently removed.
- A user who wants the markdown without inline base64 (small artifact, no `images/` folder, render later) gets text-only output and no warning that 39 images are missing — only the `Stripped 39 base64 images` log line, which sounds successful.
- The same code path is shared with non-Google-Docs sources where alt may also be empty.

## Fix

Always emit a visible placeholder. Either:

```rust
let placeholder = if alt.is_empty() {
    "![]()"           // empty image ref so renderers still render a slot
} else {
    &format!("*[image: {alt}]*")
};
```

…or, more principled: when the source is a Google Docs API export (which has no original URLs to "keep"), refuse the flag and tell the user to pick `--extract-images` or `--embed-images`.

## Reproducible test (fake data)

### Rust — `rust/tests/integration/strip_base64_empty_alt.rs`

```rust
use web_capture::extract_images::strip_base64_images;

const TINY_PNG: &str = "iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAAEAAAABCAYAAAAfFcSJAAAADUlEQVR42mP8/5+hHgAHggJ/PchI7wAAAABJRU5ErkJggg==";

#[test]
