## Summary

When a Google Doc uses **explicit hierarchical numbering in heading text** (e.g. a section titled `13. Foo` with subsections `13.1 Foo Sub-A`, `13.2 Foo Sub-B`, `13.3 Foo Sub-C`), the captured markdown should preserve that numbering verbatim, regardless of capture method or implementation language.

Today, only **`--capture api`** preserves the parent number `13.` consistently. The browser path drops it entirely, and Rust API renumbers it to a list-restart `3.`:

| Path           | Parent rendered as              | Sub `13.1` rendered as                          |
|----------------|---------------------------------|--------------------------------------------------|
| JS-API         | `13. **Foo**`                   | `**13.1** **Foo Sub-A**` (inline bold)           |
| Rust-API       | `3. **Foo**`  ❌ wrong number   | `**13.1 Foo Sub-A**` (inline bold)               |
| JS-browser     | `- **Foo**`   ❌ no number      | `> ***13.1 Foo Sub-A***` ❌ unwanted blockquote   |
| Rust-browser   | `- **Foo**`   ❌ no number      | `> ***13.1 Foo Sub-A***` ❌ unwanted blockquote   |

Three bugs in one symptom:

1. **Rust-API renumbers ordered lists** (related to issue 04 in this batch).
2. **Both browser paths convert numbered lists to bullet lists**, throwing away the user's heading number.
3. **Both browser paths wrap subsection text in `>` blockquote**, which is not in the source HTML/CSS at all (the headings are `Heading 4`/`Heading 5` in Google Docs UI).

## Why this matters

Numbered headings (`13`, `13.1`, `13.2`, ...) are how authors reference sections in cross-team conversations ("see point 13.2"). Capturing the doc and losing the numbers makes the captured copy uncitable.

Authors picking the browser path (often the recommended path for fidelity) are the most affected — they get `- Foo` with no clue that the original was `13. Foo`.

