test('inlineTableCellHtml joins multiple <p> with <br><br> for valid GFM', () => {
  const input = '<table><tr><td><p>A</p><p>B</p><p>C</p></td><td>x</td></tr></table>';
  const { html } = preprocessGoogleDocsExportHtml(input);
  // Cell should be one logical line — check that <br><br> is present and there
  // are no remaining <p> tags inside the <td>.
  expect(html).toContain('A<br><br>B<br><br>C');
  expect(html).not.toMatch(/<td[^>]*>[^<]*<p/);
});
```

## Why this matters

Tables with multi-paragraph cells are common in real-world Google Docs (FAQ-style content, translation matrices, anything with multi-language explanations). Currently any such doc round-tripped through JS api-mode produces broken markdown that no GFM renderer (GitHub, GitLab, mkdocs, pandoc, comrak, marked) will display correctly.

## Engine parity check

Rust 0.3.10 has been correct since at least 0.3.9 — its preprocessor uses the equivalent of `<br/><br/>`. The JS port simply diverged. Aligning the two CLIs on `<br><br>` makes them match.

## Summary

When converting Google Docs HTML to markdown inside the archive pipeline, any `<img>` nested inside a heading element (`<h1>`..`<h6>`) is silently dropped from the output markdown, even though:

- The image **is** extracted to `images/image-NN.ext` on disk.
- The image **is** preserved in `document.html` with `src="images/image-NN.ext"`.
- Only the markdown drops it.

Result inside a single archive:

- `document.html`: N `<img>` tags
- `images/`: N real image files
- `document.md`: N-1 `![...](images/...)` references (one heading-nested image missing)

This is the residual of bug B from #58 that remained after v0.3.0 landed. Only the Rust implementation is affected — see parity note below.

## Root cause

Two code paths with different parent-awareness:

1. Image extraction — `rust/src/gdocs.rs:257-284` — runs a **regex over raw HTML** and numbers every `<img src="data:image/...;base64,...">` in source order. Parent element is irrelevant. Always finds N.
2. Markdown conversion — `rust/src/markdown.rs:40` — calls `html2md::parse_html`. The `html2md` heading handler (for `<h1>`..`<h6>`) recurses only into text / inline-text children and does not emit `<img>` children. Heading-nested images silently vanish.

Cited lines (main branch):
- `rust/src/gdocs.rs:257-284` — `extract_base64_images` sequential numbering
- `rust/src/gdocs.rs:314-316` — rewrite → html2md handoff
- `rust/src/markdown.rs:40` — `html2md::parse_html` drop site

## Verification test (fails on main, passes once fixed)

Place at `rust/tests/gdocs_image_parity.rs`:

```rust
use web_capture::gdocs::extract_base64_images;
use web_capture::markdown::convert_html_to_markdown;

const PNG_B64: &str = "iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAAEAAAABCAQAAAC1HAwCAAAAC0lEQVR42mNkYAAAAAYAAjCB0C8AAAAASUVORK5ErkJggg==";

#[test]
fn img_inside_heading_is_kept_in_markdown() {
    let html = format!(
        r#"<html><body>
<h1><img src="data:image/png;base64,{b}" alt=""> Title With Image</h1>
<p>Body <img src="data:image/png;base64,{b}" alt=""> paragraph.</p>
</body></html>"#,
        b = PNG_B64
    );

    let (local_html, images) = extract_base64_images(&html);
    assert_eq!(images.len(), 2, "two images should be extracted to files");

    let md = convert_html_to_markdown(&local_html, None).unwrap();
    let md_refs = md.matches("](images/").count();

    assert_eq!(
        md_refs, images.len(),
        "markdown image refs ({}) must match extracted image files ({}). MD:\n{}",
        md_refs, images.len(), md
    );
}
```

On current main this prints 1 ref vs 2 files. After fix it must print 2 vs 2.

## Fix direction

Option A (minimal): pre-process HTML before `html2md::parse_html` — walk the DOM and unwrap `<img>` children of `<h1>..<h6>` into an adjacent `<p>` (or `<figure>` following the heading). Preserves reading order, satisfies html2md.

Option B (robust): replace `html2md` with a DOM-aware converter whose rules emit `![alt](src)` for every `<img>` regardless of ancestor. This also avoids the next similar bug (any other element class html2md treats as text-only).

Either way, the test above should remain green.

## JavaScript — not affected

Verified that the JS implementation is **not vulnerable** to this bug. JS uses `turndown` (`js/src/lib.js:5,157,492`) for HTML→MD conversion. Turndown's default heading rule emits `'#'.repeat(level) + ' ' + content` where `content` is the recursively-converted children, and the default `image` rule (`filter: 'img'`) emits `![alt](src)` for every `<img>` regardless of parent. Inline heading images survive as e.g. `# ![alt](...)`.

`js/src/gdocs.js:168` `extractBase64Images(html)` uses the same raw-HTML regex approach as Rust (source-ordered, parent-agnostic), but because Turndown preserves the images, the markdown ref count stays in sync with the extracted file count.

### Suggested parity regression test (JS)

Place at `js/tests/unit/heading-image.test.js`:

```js
import { convertHtmlToMarkdown } from '../../src/lib.js';
import { extractBase64Images } from '../../src/gdocs.js';

test('<img> inside <h1> survives Turndown and stays in sync with extraction', () => {
  const png = 'iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAAEAAAABCAQAAAC1HAwCAAAAC0lEQVR42mNgYAAAAAMAASsJTYQAAAAASUVORK5CYII=';
  const html = `<html><body><h1>Title <img src="data:image/png;base64,${png}" alt="x"></h1><p>Body</p></body></html>`;

  const md = convertHtmlToMarkdown(html, 'http://x');
  const mdImgCount = (md.match(/!\[[^\]]*\]\(data:image\/[^)]+\)/g) || []).length;

  const { images } = extractBase64Images(html);
  expect(mdImgCount).toBe(images.length); // both = 1
});
```

This is expected to pass today. It should be added anyway as a guard rail — if someone swaps Turndown for another library (or changes plugin config) that drops heading images, CI must catch it.

